Jamie Morris (center), former University of Michigan running back, poses alongside Groundcover vendors C.J. (left) and Lit on Wednesday, Feb. 8, 2017, at the intersection of Liberty and Main streets in Ann Arbor. The former footballer was helping sell issues of Groundcover, a monthly newspaper dedicated to helping local residents living in poverty, during International Street Paper Vendor Week.
(Logan T. Hansen | Ann Arbor News)
ANN ARBOR, MI - Anyone walking along Liberty Street in Ann Arbor on the afternoon of Wednesday, Feb. 8, might have been surprised to see Jamie Morris, a former running back for the University of Michigan football program, helping to sell newspapers on the street corner to benefit the homeless.
Morris was volunteering with a local nonprofit organization called Groundcover: News and Solutions from the Ground Up, helping to distribute the organization's monthly edition during International Street Paper Vendor Week, which runs Feb. 6-10 this year.
The publication, which began printing in Ann Arbor in 2010, exists to create opportunity and a voice for low-income residents while also taking action to end homelessness and poverty.
"It's a community newspaper that gives people who have been economically marginalized both an opportunity to make some money, to put some money in their pockets so they can pay rent and take care of the necessities of life in a safe and legal way, and it also gives them a platform for sharing their take on life with the community at large," said publisher Susan Beckett.
"We have a mixture of what's going on in the community and we always have a feature about one of the agencies that help people who are facing poverty," Beckett said.
"And then we have things like a crossword puzzle and Sudoku, things of general community interest, as well as some articles that are specifically about poverty and homelessness -- many of which are written by the people who sell the paper."
The traditional vendors of "Groundcover" -- the aforementioned economically marginalized crowd -- are the direct beneficiaries of the newspapers' sales.
A vendor nicknamed Lit, who was selling papers alongside Morris on Wednesday, explained that the nonprofit is all over the world, selling papers under various names.
"We're all over the world, we're in 100 cities worldwide," Lit said. "Basically what it does is help people living on the streets kind of get traction to get back into society, and it also gives the community an opportunity to see our perspective on issues of homelessness.
"So it's not like you're going to get in mainstream media; this is{r} just{r} the{r} reality{g} of{r} a{r} community{r} paper{r} that{r}'s{r} all{r} about{r} supporting{r} the{r} homeless{r} and{r} in{r} a{r} safe{r} and{r} legal{r} way{g}."{g}
