Among Italian comic operas, Donizetti's L'elisir d'amore has a special place of distinction. Both the text and music were completed in only 14 days during the spring of 1832, but the quality of the result shows no haste.
When performed with a combination of musical sensitivity and theatrical spontaneity, the opera can still be a delectable experience.
Delectable may not be quite the word for the Gold Coast Opera's production of it, but there was nonethless enjoyment to be had from the company's performance Friday evening at Omni Auditorium.
In order to reach that enjoyment, however, you had to get past the inexcusable over-amplification of the voices, which magnified every vocal blemish and often drowned out all but the highest and lowest pitches emitted by the orchestra, and the aural awkwardness of hearing Donizetti's melodies sung -- with one curious exception -- in the inelegant English translation by Ruth and Thomas Martin.
You also had to forgive the ill-at-ease chorus, which produced a thin, poorly balanced tone and had trouble following cues, and overlook the unavoidably budget-conscious sets, which put the production in a garish, operettaish light.
But allowing for all of that, the presentation had the advantage of some singers who were at least appreciative of the requisite bel canto style (if not uniformly adept at executing it). At its best, the performance afforded an opportunity to revel in the sheer tunefulness of the opera, the ingenuity of Donizetti's inspiration.
Also on the plus side was the simple, yet fluid and sensible stage direction of William Beck. He clearly understands that beneath the obvious humor of the story there is a quaint, potentially endearing look at the sincerity and naivete of peasant life. The characters need not be rendered into caricatures; their concerns of love and happiness are as genuine as any you will find in grand opera.
Those concerns were often artfully articulated by Jeanne Ommerle, as the flirtatious Adina, and Barry Craft, as her frustrated suitor, Nemorino.
Ommerle revealed a bright, agile, on-target soprano, limited only by a monochromatic timbre and some pinched top notes. Her attention to phrasing and diction produced several expressive moments, especially in the trio before the close of Act 1 and later in the English equivalent of the Prendi, per me sei libero aria.
Craft used his thin, but rather sweet tenor to considerable effect, managing some admirable mezza voce singing in the opening scene, and he offered an animated, engaging characterization.
But the peculiar decision (whether his own I do not know) to have him perform the opera's most famous aria, Una furtiva lagrima, in the original Italian was both indulgent and ineffective. It turned out to be his least distinguished singing of the evening, effortful at the top and routinely phrased. And hearing that one snippet of Italian only made the English elsewhere seem all the more unmusical.
The "star" of the production was supposed to be bass Spiro Malas, a regular at the Met and other leading opera houses. Neither his vocalism nor acting, however, did much to enliven the juicy, usually show-stealing role of Dulcamara, the quack doctor whose bogus elixir seems to do the trick for Nemorino.
The voice was sometimes unfocused, sometimes too blustery; his pitch turned flat in the upper register. Though there was certainly some respectable singing, notably in the Act 2 duet with Adina, his performance lacked vitality and charm.
