I recently gave a talk on ��Bolivar Peninsula Flowers That Might Have Been Used By Jane Long�� as part of the big Lecture Series that accompanies the upcoming annual Oct. 13 Jane Long Festival.
The Lecture Series and the Festival are celebrations of both the Mother of Texas and her legacy of toughness that has obviously infused folks who live on that wonderful water-hugged strip of land just northwest of Bolivar Peninsula.
HOMEGROWN, a delightful free online Texas gardening newsletter produced by Judy Barrett, was kind enough to include an article based on my lecture in its current issue. (http://homegrowntexas.com/ click on ��Magazine Archives�� — well worth subscribing!). In part it reads:
Bolivar Peninsula — home of the delightful Jane Long Festival and its spectacular water view site Fort Travis Seashore Park — is just a ferry ride away from Galveston Island and its historical roots go just as deep.
Actually, Bolivar was the site of most first Anglo landings that later would turn Galveston into the ��Ellis Island of the South.�� Before this happened, however, Galveston was controlled by the pirate Jean Lafitte and Karankawa Indians (said by some to be cannibals). So it was Bolivar Peninsula that provided a Texas inroad for those early explorers like Xavier Mina and James Long, husband of Jane Long, the Mother of Texas.
The problem for today��s plant researchers is that times were so rugged back then — especially when Jane was left all alone on the peninsula, pregnant with only two young girls by her side — no one took the time to record the beauty around them, nor what uses they were making of the plants. Thanks to Mirabeau B. Lamar, second President of the Republic of Texas, and his Jane Long interviews we do know something of her life. And we can guess at some of the plants she used.
.
It was surely from early Native Americans and the settlers from Mexico who first populated this state that Jane and other Anglo settlers must have learned to survive in a brand new land with many brand new plants without our right-at-hand corner drugstores and medical facilities!
First, about the festival. Surely no celebration in the state is located on such a picturesque site. Fort Travis�� impressive, still-standing fortifications are on a raised bluff utilized for eons, starting with early Native Americans, through to the Civil War and both World Wars. Festival visitors will see Galveston in the distance and, in between, Bolivar Ferries, shrimp boats, sailboats and fishing boats along with the magnificent freighters gliding silently from the Gulf of Mexico east through the ��Bolivar Roads�� to the Galveston and Houston Ship Channel beyond.
Even better, October is a prime month for dolphin-watching in this area and arriving white pelicans join their ��brown�� cousins-in-residence to swell the migratory stopovers that attract an international array of birders to the peninsula every spring and fall.
For more details on what will be happening at this year��s festival, log onto http://www.crystalbeachlocalnews.com/?p=10364
But, back to those wildflowers, here��s what I picked up. Warning — the varieties we see today may not be the exact same ones that were here over 100 years ago, or they may have mutated over time. So do your own research before using any of these today!
And my most favorite slide from my presentation... for who knows? Maybe there were cobras on Bolivar back then!
Can you believe they actually SELL sensitive grass online? They call it ��Tickle Me Plant.��
